byte 0x41
   cp1252     U+0041   'A'
   latin-1    U+0041   'A'
   cp1250     U+0041   'A'
   mac-roman  U+0041   'A'

byte 0xe9
   cp1252     U+00E9   'é'
   latin-1    U+00E9   'é'
   cp1250     U+00E9   'é'
   mac-roman  U+00C8   'È'

byte 0x80
   cp1252     U+20AC   '€'
   latin-1    U+0080   (a control, nothing drawn)
   cp1250     U+20AC   '€'
   mac-roman  U+00C4   'Ä'

0x41 is A in all four, and in every code page there has ever been: the
first 128 are ASCII by agreement and that is the only thing they share.

0xE9 is e-acute in three of the four and a different letter in the
fourth. That is the dangerous shape -- not a refusal, not a mess, just a
plausible wrong letter, which is why the wrong table reads like a typo
rather than like a bug.

0x80 is the one that settles the 'is cp1252 a superset of Latin-1'
question, and the answer is NO. In cp1252 it is the euro sign; in
Latin-1 it is a C1 control character with nothing to draw. cp1252
REPLACED Latin-1's top control block, so the two tables disagree about
32 positions rather than one extending the other.
